1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,782
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,802 --> 00:00:08,995
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,395 --> 00:00:15,895
This is Lecture 22, automated testing and continuous iteration.

4
00:00:16,295 --> 00:00:21,500
We start from a firmware update that made trims fail now and then,

5
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:27,597
and answer three questions. Under what conditions do we test, how many times,

6
00:00:27,617 --> 00:00:30,051
and with how realistic a model?

7
00:00:31,572 --> 00:00:40,248
A trouser factory updated its lockstitch firmware from v1.3.2 to v1.4.0.

8
00:00:40,648 --> 00:00:52,741
The new fast trim cut stop-and-trim from 4000 r/min from 0.38 seconds to about 0.22.

9
00:00:53,141 --> 00:00:54,587
Before release,

10
00:00:54,607 --> 00:01:02,796
the engineer tried dozens of trims at three to four thousand r/min on the prototype;

11
00:01:02,816 --> 00:01:04,262
not one failed.

12
00:01:04,662 --> 00:01:11,193
But on the floor operators often floor the pedal to 5000 and let go,

13
00:01:11,213 --> 00:01:14,084
and the mains voltage wanders.

14
00:01:14,484 --> 00:01:19,164
Within days, occasional failed trims were reported.

15
00:01:19,564 --> 00:01:24,224
Why didn't testing catch it, and how many trials would be enough?

16
00:01:24,624 --> 00:01:25,924
That's today.

17
00:01:27,444 --> 00:01:29,159
Here's the cause.

18
00:01:29,559 --> 00:01:38,206
The trim solenoid is commanded at 290 degrees of the trim stitch;

19
00:01:38,226 --> 00:01:45,807
knife entry is 290 plus 6nt, and past 330 the trim fails.

20
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:52,861
With a 10 millisecond delay the command speed must be at most 667 r/min.

21
00:01:52,881 --> 00:01:58,238
v1.4.0 planned braking at 100 percent of servo capability,

22
00:01:58,258 --> 00:02:05,655
but above base speed the servo enters its constant-power region and brakes less,

23
00:02:05,675 --> 00:02:09,827
and the base speed itself moves with voltage.

24
00:02:10,227 --> 00:02:19,327
Stopping from 5000, the command comes at about 514 r/min at normal voltage,

25
00:02:19,347 --> 00:02:28,083
but about 1150 when the voltage is 10 percent low: entry at 345 degrees,

26
00:02:28,103 --> 00:02:30,272
a certain failure.

27
00:02:31,792 --> 00:02:35,194
Why did hand trials miss it?

28
00:02:35,594 --> 00:02:39,814
Three reasons, and each is a problem we solve today.

29
00:02:40,214 --> 00:02:46,106
Conditions: people habitually try three to four thousand r/min,

30
00:02:46,126 --> 00:02:50,234
rarely floor it to five thousand and let go,

31
00:02:50,254 --> 00:02:53,612
and never pick a low-voltage moment.

32
00:02:54,012 --> 00:03:01,126
Count: even at 4750, with a 1.2 percent failure rate,

33
00:03:01,146 --> 00:03:09,472
fifty tries have about a 55 percent chance of showing nothing.

34
00:03:09,872 --> 00:03:16,115
Model: the SIL used an ideal motor with no constant-power region,

35
00:03:16,135 --> 00:03:19,006
so everything passed; the HIL,

36
00:03:19,026 --> 00:03:24,884
with the prototype's measured torque–speed curve, exposed it.

37
00:03:26,404 --> 00:03:30,344
Layer the tests by how far they are from the real machine.

38
00:03:30,744 --> 00:03:37,655
At the bottom, unit tests: hundreds of cases in a minute,

39
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:45,316
catching logic errors such as the wrap from 359.9 to 0 degrees.

40
00:03:45,716 --> 00:03:53,736
SIL connects the firmware to a machine model for big parameter sweeps,

41
00:03:53,756 --> 00:03:58,100
but can't see physics the model lacks.

42
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:04,524
HIL uses the real controller and driver boards on a real-time simulator,

43
00:04:04,544 --> 00:04:10,903
so interrupts, bus and solenoid delays are real, and faults can be injected.

44
00:04:11,303 --> 00:04:15,283
The full-machine bench measures what no model has,

45
00:04:15,303 --> 00:04:17,683
like stitch quality and noise.

46
00:04:18,083 --> 00:04:24,183
And end-of-line testing checks that this particular unit was assembled right.

47
00:04:24,583 --> 00:04:28,820
The rule: catch each defect at the lowest layer that can see it.

48
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:35,394
Test scripts are Python with pytest.

49
00:04:35,794 --> 00:04:40,279
Fixtures connect to the prototype, read the serial number,

50
00:04:40,299 --> 00:04:43,308
firmware version and parameter version,

51
00:04:43,328 --> 00:04:48,046
and always switch the actuators off afterwards, pass or fail;

52
00:04:48,066 --> 00:04:53,094
results go onto the bus in the WenQuest digital-factory envelope.

53
00:04:53,494 --> 00:04:58,528
Parametrisation turns one case into a set:

54
00:04:58,548 --> 00:05:06,832
the trim case runs 250 times at each of six speeds from 3000 to 5000.

55
00:05:07,232 --> 00:05:17,356
Watch the bench run v1.3.2, v1.4.0, v1.4.1 and v1.4.2 in turn.

56
00:05:17,756 --> 00:05:23,259
On v1.4.0 the trim sweep turns red;

57
00:05:23,279 --> 00:05:31,623
the regression is caught by the 4750 and 5000 points.

58
00:05:32,023 --> 00:05:36,283
Testing only at 4000 would have missed it.

59
00:05:37,803 --> 00:05:42,303
Trim success must be at least 99.5 percent.

60
00:05:42,703 --> 00:05:46,243
Two hundred straight successes: is that enough?

61
00:05:46,643 --> 00:05:47,158
No.

62
00:05:47,558 --> 00:05:52,887
In statistical terms we must show, at confidence C,

63
00:05:52,907 --> 00:05:56,138
that reliability is at least R.

64
00:05:56,538 --> 00:06:01,536
Argue by contradiction: if reliability were only R,

65
00:06:01,556 --> 00:06:06,948
n straight successes would have probability R to the n,

66
00:06:06,968 --> 00:06:11,278
and we require that to be at most 1 minus C.

67
00:06:11,678 --> 00:06:22,418
With 99.5 and 95 percent that gives 597.6, so 598 trials.

68
00:06:22,818 --> 00:06:28,296
A handy approximation is three times the reciprocal of the failure rate.

69
00:06:28,696 --> 00:06:41,067
Allowing one failure needs 947, two 1258, three 1549.

70
00:06:42,587 --> 00:06:46,389
What if x of the n trials fail?

71
00:06:46,789 --> 00:06:50,640
The point estimate is 1 minus x over n,

72
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:55,106
but decisions use the lower confidence bound.

73
00:06:55,506 --> 00:06:59,101
Failures follow a binomial distribution;

74
00:06:59,121 --> 00:07:05,607
the failure rate at which 'at most x failures' has probability exactly 1

75
00:07:05,627 --> 00:07:08,046
minus C is the upper bound.

76
00:07:08,446 --> 00:07:09,946
That's the Clopper–Pearson method.

77
00:07:10,346 --> 00:07:17,486
On the right, the bench calculator cycles through 0, 1,

78
00:07:17,506 --> 00:07:20,741
2 and 3 failures in 1000.

79
00:07:21,141 --> 00:07:27,983
One failure: lower bound 99.53 percent, which passes.

80
00:07:28,383 --> 00:07:35,423
Two failures: the estimate of 99.8 percent looks fine,

81
00:07:35,443 --> 00:07:39,083
but the bound is only 99.37.

82
00:07:39,483 --> 00:07:42,899
So 1000 trials can allow at most one failure.

83
00:07:44,419 --> 00:07:49,704
Continuous integration asks a second question: if it got worse,

84
00:07:49,724 --> 00:07:52,399
how many trials until we see it?

85
00:07:52,799 --> 00:08:00,499
The chance of at least one failure is 1 minus (1 minus p) to the n.

86
00:08:00,899 --> 00:08:12,620
At 4750 r/min, with a 1.2 percent failure rate, 250 trials give 95 percent;

87
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:21,544
at 5000, with 17 percent, just 10 trials give 84 percent.

88
00:08:21,944 --> 00:08:29,866
To be 95 percent sure of catching a 0.5 percent regression takes 598 trials,

89
00:08:29,886 --> 00:08:34,151
the same formula as zero-failure testing.

90
00:08:34,551 --> 00:08:41,171
The principle: spend your limited trials on the conditions most likely to fail.

91
00:08:42,691 --> 00:08:48,891
Stop error is a continuous measurement, so we can use a variables index.

92
00:08:49,291 --> 00:08:54,676
It has only an upper limit, 1 degree, so we use one-sided Cpk:

93
00:08:54,696 --> 00:08:59,471
upper limit minus mean, over three standard deviations.

94
00:08:59,871 --> 00:09:06,891
At least 1.33 means four sigma between the mean and the limit.

95
00:09:07,291 --> 00:09:15,274
Our case hides a second problem: v1.3.2 raised the position gain to 300,

96
00:09:15,294 --> 00:09:21,053
and a single stop at 0.91 degrees passed every time.

97
00:09:21,453 --> 00:09:30,367
Switch to 50 stops and Cpk is only 0.34; the maximum, 1.13 degrees,

98
00:09:30,387 --> 00:09:34,233
was over the limit all along.

99
00:09:35,753 --> 00:09:38,554
When is a prototype finished?

100
00:09:38,954 --> 00:09:44,752
Leave it to gut feeling and it stays at 'almost' forever.

101
00:09:45,152 --> 00:09:53,951
Electronics uses a ladder of maturity stages: P0 runs, P1 has all features,

102
00:09:53,971 --> 00:10:00,653
then engineering validation, EVT; design validation, DVT;

103
00:10:00,673 --> 00:10:06,415
production validation, PVT; then mass production.

104
00:10:06,815 --> 00:10:13,075
Here each stage's exit criteria are written as numbers an automated test can decide.

105
00:10:13,475 --> 00:10:18,202
EVT, for example: stop Cpk at least 1.33,

106
00:10:18,222 --> 00:10:23,759
at most one failure in 1000 trims at each speed,

107
00:10:23,779 --> 00:10:27,695
and ten green HIL nights in a row.

108
00:10:28,095 --> 00:10:30,515
Reviews look only at the data.

109
00:10:32,035 --> 00:10:37,155
Robot arm and mobile robot software is tested on the same pyramid:

110
00:10:37,175 --> 00:10:41,282
unit tests for inverse kinematics and state machines,

111
00:10:41,302 --> 00:10:44,943
SIL with the controller on a physics simulator,

112
00:10:44,963 --> 00:10:51,095
HIL with real joint drivers on a motor simulator, and only then the real robot.

113
00:10:51,495 --> 00:10:54,836
Today's regression has a near-twin there:

114
00:10:54,856 --> 00:10:59,181
plan trajectories at 100 percent of motor capability,

115
00:10:59,201 --> 00:11:05,494
and when the battery sags the base speed drops and fast segments fall behind;

116
00:11:05,514 --> 00:11:11,315
only HIL with measured torque–speed curves, or the real robot, sees it.

117
00:11:11,715 --> 00:11:21,295
And proving 99.5 percent grasp success also takes 598 straight successes.

118
00:11:22,815 --> 00:11:25,320
Everyday life works the same way.

119
00:11:25,720 --> 00:11:32,753
A new umbrella that kept out a few drizzles tells you nothing about a gale

120
00:11:32,773 --> 00:11:34,182
and a downpour.

121
00:11:34,582 --> 00:11:40,221
To test it, pick the worst weather on purpose, and try enough times.

122
00:11:40,621 --> 00:11:46,530
How many times and under what conditions matter far more than the words 'we

123
00:11:46,550 --> 00:11:47,321
tried it'.

124
00:11:48,841 --> 00:11:50,133
Back to the start.

125
00:11:50,533 --> 00:11:53,737
With a 10 millisecond delay,

126
00:11:53,757 --> 00:11:59,724
a command speed above 667 r/min means a failed trim,

127
00:11:59,744 --> 00:12:07,553
and v1.4.0 stopping from 5000 at low voltage commands at about 1150.

128
00:12:07,953 --> 00:12:17,693
Someone trying 50 times at 4750 has a 55 percent chance of seeing no failure at all.

129
00:12:18,093 --> 00:12:26,924
Proving 99.5 percent at 95 percent confidence takes 598 straight successes.

130
00:12:27,324 --> 00:12:37,795
Testing 250 times a night at 4750 catches it with about 95 percent probability;

131
00:12:37,815 --> 00:12:42,576
at 5000 a handful of trials will do.

132
00:12:42,976 --> 00:12:47,227
The fix plans at 90 percent of the measured curve,

133
00:12:47,247 --> 00:12:50,986
with a backstop that trims one stitch later.

134
00:12:52,506 --> 00:12:56,481
Now Virtual lab 22-1, the automated test bench,

135
00:12:56,501 --> 00:13:02,346
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. Tasks and results are blurred.

136
00:13:02,366 --> 00:13:08,722
Pick a version, a level and cases on the left; top right is the change log.

137
00:13:09,122 --> 00:13:15,366
First choose v1.4.0 and software-in-the-loop, and run the default set.

138
00:13:15,386 --> 00:13:19,661
With an ideal motor model, all three cases pass.

139
00:13:20,061 --> 00:13:24,804
Switch to hardware-in-the-loop and run the default set again.

140
00:13:24,824 --> 00:13:28,864
With trimming fixed at 4000 r/min and a single stop,

141
00:13:28,884 --> 00:13:34,642
all seven cases are green too: exactly how the regression slipped through.

142
00:13:35,041 --> 00:13:40,789
Task one: tick only the trim speed sweep and run it on v1.3.2 first.

143
00:13:40,809 --> 00:13:45,199
Read the failures at each speed and the lower bound,

144
00:13:45,219 --> 00:13:51,645
then compare the other versions to find which one introduced the regression,

145
00:13:51,665 --> 00:13:54,020
and which requirement fails.

146
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:59,689
Task two: back to the default set on v1.4.0, add the trim sweep,

147
00:13:59,709 --> 00:14:04,233
and the budget bar turns red, over 240 minutes a night.

148
00:14:04,253 --> 00:14:10,513
What would you swap so that fault injection stays and both the trim and stop

149
00:14:10,533 --> 00:14:12,414
regressions are caught?

150
00:14:12,434 --> 00:14:13,240
Your call.

151
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:19,203
Then switch to the machine test bench, run v1.4.0 and v1.4.2,

152
00:14:19,223 --> 00:14:26,067
and compare the worst-point trim failure rate and the maximum stop error in

153
00:14:26,087 --> 00:14:28,081
the trend chart below.

154
00:14:28,101 --> 00:14:32,932
The results are blurred; run it yourself and compare.

155
00:14:33,332 --> 00:14:34,297
Task three:

156
00:14:34,317 --> 00:14:41,015
in the calculator enter 99.5 percent reliability and 95 percent confidence,

157
00:14:41,035 --> 00:14:46,928
with 0 and then 1 failure allowed, and read off the trials needed,

158
00:14:46,948 --> 00:14:51,675
then the lower bound for 0, 1 and 2 failures in 1000.

